A worked `acked_implies_durable` example — a toy that writes one byte per
round and only claims the ack once its `sync` returns `Ok`:

```rust
use charpente::core::io::Disk;
use charpente_sim::Sim;
use charpente_sim::checks::{acked_implies_durable, is_fault_fallout};

/// Returns a fresh `drive` closure: each call performs one round, writing
/// the next byte and syncing before claiming its ack — never before.
fn drive() -> impl FnMut(&mut Sim) -> Result<Vec<String>, String> {
    let mut round: u64 = 0;
    move |sim: &mut Sim| {
        let file = match sim.disk().open("bytes") {
            Ok(f) => f,
            Err(e) if is_fault_fallout(&e.to_string()) => return Ok(vec![]),
            Err(e) => return Err(e.to_string()),
        };
        if let Err(e) = file.write_at(round, &[0xAA]) {
            let msg = e.to_string();
            return if is_fault_fallout(&msg) { Ok(vec![]) } else { Err(msg) };
        }
        if let Err(e) = file.sync() {
            let msg = e.to_string();
            return if is_fault_fallout(&msg) { Ok(vec![]) } else { Err(msg) };
        }
        let id = format!("byte-{round}");
        round += 1;
        Ok(vec![id])
    }
}

fn verify(sim: &mut Sim, acked: &[String]) -> Result<(), String> {
    let file = sim.disk().open("bytes").map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let len = file.len().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    for id in acked {
        let offset: u64 = id
            .strip_prefix("byte-")
            .expect("ack ids are \"byte-N\"")
            .parse()
            .expect("ack ids carry a numeric offset");
        if offset >= len {
            return Err(format!("{id} missing after restart (file len {len})"));
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

fn bytes_survive_restarts(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<(), String> {
    // `sim`, not `sim.seed()`: the checker restarts the world you hand it, so
    // the `restart` coverage lands where the harness reads it and your
    // run's fault config is the one the property runs under.
    acked_implies_durable(sim, 5, drive(), verify)
}
```

### Driving the whole app in a sim

The workloads above drive `Db` directly. The full app — routes, jobs, and
cron multiplexed on one loop — is just as drivable, because the loop itself
(`charpente::kernel::Kernel`) sits inside the deterministic boundary:
`charpente::serve` (§5) runs it against hyper's channel in production; a
sim test runs the identical loop code against a scripted timeline.
`charpente_sim::drive` runs that loop through the four steps of "A fault-free
tail for an end-of-run check" above, and the example below is built on it.
Its tail re-sends what went unanswered, so every route a workload sends must
be safe to receive twice (that section, "A route the tail re-sends must be
safe to receive twice"). `hit` below takes the re-send by design: it counts
landings, and both of its invariants are stated over jobs, so a hit that
lands twice is two Done jobs and two hits.

**Requests** are built with the public builder: `Request::new(Method::Post,
"/hit")`, then `.header(key, value)` (keys are lowercased for you) and
`.json(&value)` for a JSON body. An authenticated request carries its
session token (§5) the same way production traffic does:
`.header("authorization", format!("Bearer {token}"))`. `Drive::faulted_phase`
takes each with the offset it arrives at, counted from the instant the app
booted.

**The end state.** `recovered.kernel` is the tail's kernel, which keeps its
`App`. `kernel.app().db().snapshot()` reads the durable end-state, and
`kernel.stats()` snapshots the loop's counters for the tail's run
(`requests`, `jobs_done`, `jobs_retried`, `jobs_dead`, `cron_ticks`,
`cron_catchups`, and §6's outbound counters). A tenant's
rows read through `snap.scan_scoped::<R>(tenant)`
and `snap.get_scoped(tenant, id)`, where `tenant` is its raw id (§16). The job
queue reads through `snap.scan::<JobRow>()`; `charpente::core::job::JobRow`'s
fields are all `pub`: `id: Id<JobRow>`, `name: String`, `payload: Vec<u8>`,
`key: Option<String>`, `status: JobStatus` (`Pending`, `Done` or `Dead`),
`attempts: u32`, `due_at: WallTime` (`charpente::core::io::WallTime`;
`WallTime::UNIX_EPOCH` means due now), `last_error: Option<String>`, `shape:
Option<u64>`, `scope: Option<u64>` (the tenant's raw id; `None` for a global
job) and `outbound_key: Option<[u8; 16]>` (`Some` on an outbound job: the
bytes of the key its calls carry, as a `Delivered`'s `key.as_bytes()`
returns them, below).

One mindset note before the example: under `charpente test --sim`, every
run gets its own swarm-sampled fault matrix, so exact-trace assertions
("exactly 3 requests, exactly 5 jobs done") will fail whenever a fault
fires. Assert *invariants* — claims that hold under any fault schedule.
`Drive` classifies the kernel's own failures. After the tail, the disk's
faults are off, so an error in the end-state check is a real failure. A
workload below the helper classifies its own with `is_fault_fallout`, like
the `Db`-level workload above, plus `clock_fallout(sim, text)` for a clock
stop or refusal, which `is_fault_fallout` alone never excuses ("Wall time and
its faults"). (For an exact-trace test, build your own
fault-free world inside the workload: `Sim::with_faults(seed,
FaultConfig::none())`.)

```rust
use charpente::core::job::{JobRow, JobStatus};
use charpente::core::table::{Codec, Id};
use charpente::http::{ApiError, App, AppConfig, Method, Request};
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::wire::WireU64;
use charpente::{Schema, Tx, View, Wire, job, jobs, route, routes, table};
use charpente_sim::Sim;
use charpente_sim::drive::Drive;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::time::Duration;

#[table(global)]
struct Counter {
    id: Id<Counter>,
    hits: u64,
}

// A job payload never crosses HTTP, so it has no `Wire` requirement — this
// stays a bare `u64`.
#[job(global)]
struct CountUp {
    slot: u64,
}

impl CountUp {
    fn run(self, tx: &mut Tx) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        let hits = tx.get(Id::<Counter>::from_raw(self.slot))?.map_or(0, |c| c.hits);
        tx.put(&Counter {
            id: Id::from_raw(self.slot),
            hits: hits + 1,
        });
        Ok(())
    }
}

// A route body IS a wire type, so `slot` is `WireU64` here even though the
// job payload above carries the same value as a bare `u64`.
#[derive(Wire, Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct HitBody {
    slot: WireU64,
}

// Takes the tail's re-send by design: a hit that lands twice is two jobs, and
// the invariants below count jobs. A route whose property is exactly-once
// answers a row that already landed instead (GUIDE §8, "A route the tail
// re-sends must be safe to receive twice").
#[route(POST "/hit", public)]
fn hit(tx: &mut Tx, body: HitBody) -> Result<WireU64, ApiError> {
    tx.enqueue(CountUp { slot: body.slot.0 });
    Ok(body.slot)
}

fn build_app(sim: &Sim) -> std::io::Result<App> {
    Ok(App::open(
        Box::new(sim.disk()),
        Schema::new().table::<Counter>(),
        Box::new(sim.entropy()),
        AppConfig::default(),
    )?
    .mount(routes![hit])
    .mount_jobs(jobs![CountUp])
    .schedule("hourly-count", Duration::from_secs(3600), || CountUp {
        slot: 777,
    }))
}

/// The end-state oracle, run after the run. Two invariants that hold
/// under ANY fault schedule:
///
/// - Ack-after-durable, app-level: every 200 from `/hit` committed its
///   enqueue in the same log record, so at least `acked` request-slot
///   CountUp rows exist. `>=`, not `==`: a request that got a 500 for a
///   platform Io failure is ambiguous (§4) — its record may have landed
///   anyway, and the tail sends it again.
/// - Exactly-once (§6), checked from the outside: per slot, counter hits
///   ≡ Done job rows — a handler's effects and its Done-mark commit as one
///   record, so no fault schedule can split them.
fn check_invariants(app: &App, acked: u64) -> Result<(), String> {
    let snap = app.db().snapshot().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    let mut request_jobs = 0u64;
    let mut done: BTreeMap<u64, u64> = BTreeMap::new();
    for job in snap.scan::<JobRow>().map_err(|e| e.to_string())? {
        if job.name != "CountUp" {
            continue;
        }
        let slot = u64::decode(&mut &job.payload[..]).map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
        if slot < 3 {
            request_jobs += 1; // enqueued by a request, not by cron's slot 777
        }
        if job.status == JobStatus::Done {
            *done.entry(slot).or_insert(0) += 1;
        }
    }
    if request_jobs < acked {
        return Err(format!(
            "ack-durability violated: {acked} 200s acked but only {request_jobs} request jobs durable"
        ));
    }
    for counter in snap.scan::<Counter>().map_err(|e| e.to_string())? {
        let done_count = done.get(&counter.id.raw()).copied().unwrap_or(0);
        if counter.hits != done_count {
            return Err(format!(
                "exactly-once violated at slot {}: {} hits vs {done_count} Done jobs",
                counter.id.raw(),
                counter.hits
            ));
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

/// One whole-app run: three requests arrive over three virtual seconds
/// under the run's faults, each enqueueing a job; the fault-free tail runs
/// two hours, so the hourly schedule ticks twice and every job drains; then
/// the durable end-state is checked.
fn whole_app(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<(), String> {
    let requests: Vec<(Duration, Request)> = (0..3u64)
        .map(|slot| {
            (
                Duration::from_secs(slot),
                Request::new(Method::Post, "/hit").json(&HitBody { slot: WireU64(slot) }),
            )
        })
        .collect();
    let drive = Drive::new(sim, build_app);
    // Steps 1 and 2: fault fallout ends the phase, never the run, and a first
    // boot lost to it leaves the phase empty.
    let phase = drive.faulted_phase(&requests, Duration::from_secs(10))?;
    if phase.answers.iter().any(Option::is_some) {
        sim.cover("kernel:served"); // pair with `require ["kernel:served"]`
    }
    // Steps 3 and 4: a power cycle, then a fault-free tail that re-sends what
    // went unanswered and runs two hours past its boot.
    let Some(recovered) = drive.recover(&phase, Duration::from_secs(2 * 3600 + 1))? else {
        return Ok(()); // given up by name, on a durable refusal
    };
    // Only the phase's 200s are acks a client saw before the crash; the
    // tail's answers to what it re-sent are `recovered.resent`.
    let acked = (0..requests.len())
        .filter(|&i| phase.status(i) == Some(200))
        .count() as u64;
    check_invariants(recovered.kernel.app(), acked)
}
```

Register it like any workload — add `whole_app require ["kernel:served"]`
to the `sim_tests! { ... }` list.

**Below the helper.** These are the pieces `Drive` is built from, for a
workload that needs more than one faulted phase and one tail ("When to drop
below the helper" above):

- **`SimSource`** is the scripted request source.
  `SimSource::new(Box::new(sim.clock()), horizon_nanos)` bounds the
  run at a virtual-time horizon; `push_at(at_nanos, request)`
  schedules arrivals (in arrival order — pushing out of order is an
  assert). Both times are absolute virtual nanoseconds on the run's
  clock, not offsets: a source built mid-run adds
  `sim.now().as_nanos()`. `responses()` hands back a shared handle to the recorded
  `(status, body)` pairs, filled in as the kernel replies: one entry per
  request the kernel served (an SSE upgrade's opening response included), in
  arrival order. A request it never served, past the horizon or after `run`
  stopped on a poisoned engine, records nothing, so the entries are a prefix
  of the script and entry `i` answers the `i`-th push. `answers()` hands back
  the same replies by slot, one per `push_at` in push order: slot `i` is the
  `i`-th push's own `(status, body)`, and `None` until that request is
  answered, so a run that stopped says which requests it never reached.
- **`Kernel::new(app, Box::new(sim.clock()))`** consumes the mounted `App`
  (upserting cron rows and rebuilding the job due-heap — §6, §7);
  `kernel.run(&mut source)` then serves scripted requests at their virtual
  arrival times, runs due jobs, and ticks cron, exactly as in production —
  and since sleeping through an empty stretch just advances the virtual
  clock, a two-hour run finishes in milliseconds. `run` returns
  `Ok(())` once nothing is left inside the horizon, `Err` if the engine
  poisoned mid-run (§9), or on a clock stop ("Wall time and its faults"). The kernel
  keeps its `App` for life: building it moved the app's job definitions,
  outbound hosts and cron entries into the kernel, so there is no second
  kernel on the same `App`. To go on, `run` the same kernel again against
  another `SimSource`, or restart (`sim.restart_process()`, or
  `sim.restart()` for a power cycle), rebuild the app and build a new
  kernel; the next paragraph says when each fits. A job
  enqueued between two runs of one kernel, through
  `kernel.app_mut_for_tests().transact_for_tests(|tx| …)`, is picked up when
  the next `run` starts. An app that mounts an outbound job builds its kernel with
  `Kernel::with_outbound` instead (§6, "When a call fails").

Nothing limits a run to one script: `run` returns once nothing is left
inside the horizon, and the same kernel can
be `run` again against a second `SimSource` — script a login phase, read
the token out of its recorded responses, then script the authenticated
phase with `.header("authorization", format!("Bearer {token}"))`. A kernel
whose `run` returned `Err` has stopped and is never `run` again: restart
(`sim.restart_process()`, or `sim.restart()` for a power cycle), rebuild the
app and open a new `Kernel`, as production restarts a stopped process.

### The outside world: remote models

Outbound HTTP goes through the `Outbound` IO trait, to hosts the app
declares. In a sim, each declared host is a model you register: a `Remote`
that lives inside the run, runs on its seed, and answers each request
when the request reaches it. The wire between the kernel and the model
belongs to the sim, and the outbound fault axes act on that wire.

Register one model per declared host, then build the outbound handle:

- `sim.remote(host, model)` makes `model` the outside world behind `host`.
  One model per host: registering a host twice is a contract panic,
  `contract: sim.remote("<host>", …) registered twice`.
- `sim.outbound(hosts)` builds one process's handle, a
  `charpente_sim::outbound::SimOutbound`. `hosts` is the app's declared host
  list, passed as plain data. A host with no model is refused by name before
  anything runs:

<!-- pinned -->
```text
sim: a mounted job calls <host>, which has no model — add sim.remote("<host>", …) before sim.outbound(…)
```

The same text is a panic when a handle starts a call to a host that has no
model, such as one the list given to `sim.outbound` left out.

A model implements `charpente_sim::outbound::Remote`, whose one method is
`fn handle(&mut self, call: &OutboundCall, rng: &mut Rng) -> OutboundReply`,
with `OutboundCall` and `OutboundReply` from `charpente::core::io` and `Rng`
from `charpente_sim::rng`. `call` is the request as the kernel built it:
`host: &'static str`, `method: HttpMethod`, `path: String` (path and query),
`headers: Vec<(String, String)>`, `body: Vec<u8>` and `key: IdempotencyKey`.
`OutboundReply` has no constructor: build it as a struct literal of its three
`pub` fields, `status: u16`, `headers: Vec<(String, String)>` and `body:
Vec<u8>` (the example below). `handle` runs when the request reaches the
remote, at its drawn delivery time, not when the kernel starts the call.
`rng` is the model's own seeded stream, keyed by the host's name, so a
model's variance (a payment that is sometimes declined, an LLM that sometimes
answers ambiguously) replays with the seed and moves no other draw. It offers `next_u64()`, `fill(&mut [u8])`,
`chance(p)` (true with probability `p`, which must lie in [0, 1]), `below(n)`
(uniform in `0..n`) and `between(lo, hi)` (uniform in `lo..=hi`). A model is
`Send + 'static`. It runs under the sim's world lock, so it must never call
back into the `Sim`.

There are three ways to get a model. Each is a named choice, and none is a
default:

- `charpente_sim::outbound::from_fn(f)` wraps a closure,
  `FnMut(&OutboundCall) -> OutboundReply`. The closure gets no `rng`, so
  write a `Remote` impl when the model needs variance.
- `Recorder::new()` answers 200 with an empty body and keeps every call it
  was handed, in delivery order. `calls()` reads them. It models
  fire-and-forget email.
- `Idempotent::header(name, model)` wraps an inner model in Stripe's
  idempotency semantics, keyed by the value of the header `name` (matched
  case-insensitively). `Idempotent::by(extract, model)` takes the key from
  any part of the request, through `extract: fn(&OutboundCall) ->
  Option<String>`. A repeated key with the same method, path and body gets
  the first reply again, and the inner model does not run. A repeated key
  with a different method, path or body gets a 409. A request with no key is
  applied every time, as Stripe does. `applied()` counts applications per
  key. A repeated key replays, so every count is 1, and the map says which
  keys reached the inner model: a retry that sent a fresh key shows up as a
  second entry, never as a 2. `inner()` reads the wrapped model. The key is
  what the app put in the request, never `OutboundCall::key`: a real remote
  never sees that field.

A workload reads model state with `sim.with_remote::<M, _>(host, |m| …)`. The
closure borrows the model as its concrete type `M`, since a reference cannot
outlive the world lock. The closure runs under that lock, as a model does, so
it must not call the `Sim` either: return what you need and act on it after.
Naming a host with no model, or the wrong type, is a contract panic, and the
wrong-type text names both the type asked for and the one registered. A
wrapped model is read as its wrapper: `|m: &Idempotent<StripeModel>|`, then
`m.inner()`.

The closure gets `&M`, never `&mut M`, so nothing reaches a model through
`with_remote`. A model the workload must change mid-run, such as one
calmed for the fault-free tail, shares state with it from registration: the
workload keeps a clone of an `Arc<AtomicBool>` and flips it between kernel
runs. The model reads it when a request reaches it, so a request already on
the wire meets the new state. This model does that, and draws its variance
from `rng`:

```rust
use charpente::core::io::{OutboundCall, OutboundReply};
use charpente_sim::Sim;
use charpente_sim::outbound::Remote;
use charpente_sim::rng::Rng;
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};

/// An LLM that labels a message, and answers "unsure" one call in ten until
/// the workload calms it.
struct Classifier {
    calm: Arc<AtomicBool>,
}

impl Remote for Classifier {
    fn handle(&mut self, call: &OutboundCall, rng: &mut Rng) -> OutboundReply {
        let label = if !self.calm.load(Ordering::Relaxed) && rng.chance(0.1) {
            "unsure"
        } else if call.body.windows(6).any(|w| w == b"urgent") {
            "urgent"
        } else {
            "routine"
        };
        OutboundReply {
            status: 200,
            headers: vec![("content-type".to_string(), "application/json".to_string())],
            body: format!(r#"{{"label":"{label}"}}"#).into_bytes(),
        }
    }
}

/// Registers the model and hands back its switch. Before the fault-free tail
/// the workload calls `calm.store(true, Ordering::Relaxed)`.
fn register(sim: &Sim) -> Arc<AtomicBool> {
    let calm = Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false));
    sim.remote("llm.example", Classifier { calm: Arc::clone(&calm) });
    calm
}
```

`sim.outbound_deliveries()` returns every request that has reached a
remote, as `charpente_sim::outbound::Delivered { host: String, key:
IdempotencyKey, at: u64, handle: u64 }`.
`key` is the job's `OutboundCall::key`, which attributes a delivered call to
its job. `at` is the virtual arrival time in nanoseconds, and `handle` says
which `SimOutbound`, and so which process, sent it. Like `with_remote`, it
first runs every delivery due by now, so what it shows is current with the
clock.

A request in flight at a crash still arrives; only its reply is lost. Models
belong to the run, not to the box, so `sim.restart()` leaves them and
every request already on the wire alone. The restarted process builds a new
handle with `sim.outbound(…)`. A reply goes only to the handle that started
its call, so a pre-restart reply has nowhere to land, even when the new
process reuses the old token. A cancelled call (`Outbound::cancel`) behaves
the same way: its request still reaches the remote at its delivery time, and
its completion is never handed back.

Every call rolls each fault axis once, from that axis's own keyed stream,
whatever the other axes decide, and draws both of its latencies the same
way. So turning one axis on or off moves no other axis's draw. It can still
change what happens downstream: a call it refuses never reaches the model,
so the model's own `rng` sees one call fewer. Delivery and reply times are
drawn when the call starts. The call's fate is chosen from its rolls in a
fixed precedence: refused, else lost, else throttled, else delivered. A
delivered call may also be slow, and may lose its reply. Coverage counts
only what applies, so a refused call that also rolled a lost reply counts as
refused:

| Axis (`FaultConfig` field) | The remote sees | The kernel sees | Coverage key |
|---|---|---|---|
| `outbound_refused` | nothing | an immediate transport error, `ConnectionRefused` | `fault:outbound_refused` |
| `outbound_lost_request` | nothing | silence, then its deadline | `fault:outbound_lost_request` |
| `outbound_lost_reply` | the call, applied | silence, then its deadline: the ambiguous case | `fault:outbound_lost_reply` |
| `outbound_throttle` | nothing (overloaded) | a 429 or 503 | `fault:outbound_throttle` |
| `outbound_latency` `(min, max)` and `outbound_slow` | the call, late | a reply late, or past the deadline (the LLM tail) | `fault:outbound_slow`; latency has none |

`outbound_latency` is a one-way range, drawn once for the request and once
for the reply, and a throttle answers after twice the request's draw.
`outbound_slow` adds think time between the request's arrival and the
reply's departure, uniform in [10·max, 100·max], or in [1 s, 100 s] when max
is zero. Every
completion handed back to the kernel also marks `outbound:completed`.
Duplicates are not an axis. In HTTP a duplicate is a retry after a lost
reply, and the sim produces those. Whether a duplicate applies twice is the
model's semantics, which is what `Idempotent` is for.

`FaultConfig::default()`, which `Sim::new` uses, turns the five outbound
faults off and sets latency to 1–50 ms. `FaultConfig::none()` makes every
call instantaneous. A swarm run samples all six fields in
`sample_extended_fields`, each from its own stream, so the existing axes'
sampled values are unchanged:

| Field | Swarm sample |
|---|---|
| `outbound_refused` | on with chance 0.25, at a rate from 0 to 0.049 in steps of 0.001 |
| `outbound_lost_request` | on with chance 0.25, at 0 to 0.029 |
| `outbound_lost_reply` | on with chance 0.25, at 0 to 0.029 |
| `outbound_throttle` | on with chance 0.25, at 0 to 0.099 |
| `outbound_slow` | on with chance 0.25, at 0 to 0.049 |
| `outbound_latency` | `(0, max)`, with max one of 0, 10 ms, 200 ms or 2 s, each with chance 0.25 |

### Calling the outside world in a sim

An app with an outbound job is driven the way "Driving the whole app in a sim"
drives any app, with one addition: a model behind each declared host,
registered with `sim.remote` before `Drive::faulted_phase`. `Drive` builds each
kernel with `Kernel::with_outbound` over the sim's handle exactly when the app
declares hosts, and quiesces the outbound faults in the tail. Below the
helper, build the handle before the kernel, since
`sim.outbound(&app.outbound_hosts())` borrows the app that
`Kernel::with_outbound` takes. The workload below takes two orders under the
run's faults, then runs the fault-free tail ("A fault-free tail" above) and
calls the three outbound checkers:

```rust
use charpente::core::job::JobRow;
use charpente::core::table::Id;
use charpente::http::{ApiError, App, AppConfig, Method, Request};
use charpente::kernel::JobError;
use charpente::outbound::{Call, Host, IdempotencyKey, Reply};
use charpente::wire::WireU64;
use charpente::{Schema, Tx, View, Wire, job, jobs, route, routes, table};
use charpente_sim::Sim;
use charpente_sim::checks::{
    calls_have_durable_jobs, durable_corruption_fired, keys_are_per_job,
    outbound_settles_by_horizon,
};
use charpente_sim::drive::Drive;
use charpente_sim::outbound::{Idempotent, Recorder};
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::time::Duration;

#[table(global)]
struct Receipt {
    id: Id<Receipt>,
    sent: bool,
}

#[job(
    global,
    outbound = "mail.example",
    retry(max = 3, backoff = "1s"),
    timeout = "5s"
)]
struct SendReceipt {
    order: u64,
}

impl SendReceipt {
    fn call(&self, _tx: &Tx, key: &IdempotencyKey) -> Result<Option<Call>, JobError> {
        Ok(Some(
            Call::post("/send")
                .header("idempotency-key", key.as_str())
                .form(&[("order", self.order.to_string())]),
        ))
    }

    fn settle(self, tx: &mut Tx, reply: Reply) -> Result<(), JobError> {
        match reply.status() {
            200 => {
                tx.put(&Receipt {
                    id: Id::from_raw(self.order),
                    sent: true,
                });
                Ok(())
            }
            s => Err(JobError::app(format!("mail.example answered {s}"))),
        }
    }
}

#[derive(Wire, Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct OrderBody {
    order: WireU64,
}

#[route(POST "/order", public)]
fn order(tx: &mut Tx, body: OrderBody) -> Result<WireU64, ApiError> {
    let id: Id<Receipt> = Id::from_raw(body.order.0);
    // The tail re-sends an order the phase left unanswered: one that landed
    // anyway is answered as placed, with no second receipt. The request is
    // the order's id alone, so a repeat is always the same request.
    if tx.get(id)?.is_some() {
        return Ok(body.order);
    }
    tx.put(&Receipt { id, sent: false });
    tx.enqueue(SendReceipt {
        order: body.order.0,
    });
    Ok(body.order)
}

fn build_app(sim: &Sim) -> std::io::Result<App> {
    Ok(App::open(
        Box::new(sim.disk()),
        Schema::new().table::<Receipt>(),
        Box::new(sim.entropy()),
        AppConfig::default(),
    )?
    .mount(routes![order])
    .mount_jobs(jobs![SendReceipt])
    .outbound(Host::new("mail.example")))
}

fn receipts_settle_under_faults(sim: &mut Sim) -> Result<(), String> {
    sim.remote(
        "mail.example",
        Idempotent::header("idempotency-key", Recorder::new()),
    );
    let requests: Vec<(Duration, Request)> = (0..2u64)
        .map(|order| {
            (
                Duration::from_secs(order),
                Request::new(Method::Post, "/order").json(&OrderBody {
                    order: WireU64(order),
                }),
            )
        })
        .collect();
    let drive = Drive::new(sim, build_app);
    let phase = drive.faulted_phase(&requests, Duration::from_secs(30))?;
    // The tail's power cycle may find a call in flight; the job is still
    // Pending, and its next call carries the same key. 600 s is past every
    // backoff and deadline, with room to spare.
    let Some(recovered) = drive.recover(&phase, Duration::from_secs(600))? else {
        return Ok(());
    };

    let snap = recovered
        .kernel
        .app()
        .db()
        .snapshot()
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    keys_are_per_job(&snap)?;
    outbound_settles_by_horizon(&snap)?;
    if let Err(e) = calls_have_durable_jobs(sim, &snap) {
        if durable_corruption_fired(sim) {
            sim.give_up("media corruption cut an enqueue whose call was made", e);
            return Ok(());
        }
        return Err(e);
    }
    let jobs = snap.scan::<JobRow>().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
    if jobs.iter().any(|j| j.outbound_key.is_some()) {
        sim.cover("outbound:checked"); // no order committed, nothing checked
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Register it as `receipts_settle_under_faults require ["outbound:checked"]`. A
run in which no order committed passes all three checkers having checked
nothing, which is why the coverage key is marked only where an outbound job
exists. The tail places again what the phase left unanswered, so such a run
is rare. `Drive`'s faulted phase is one kernel: under a swarm's `sync_error`,
a kernel can poison early and `run` stops, leaving the rest of the phase's
script for the tail. A workload that wants more of its work done under
faults drops below the helper and runs the phase as a supervisor loop: restart
the process (`sim.restart_process()`, or `sim.restart()` once
`sim.disk_crashed()`), boot through `boot_through_fallout`, build a fresh
handle and kernel, and re-send in a new `SimSource` every request the last one
left unanswered, until the phase's time is up. Read that from the last
source's `answers()`, slot by slot, by the rule `Drive` applies: a `None`
slot, or a 5xx whose body is fault fallout
(`is_fault_fallout(t) || clock_fallout(sim, t)`). A 4xx, or a handler's own
5xx, is the app's answer and is not re-sent, so counting `responses()` to find
where the script stopped is not enough.

**Why the key matters.** The platform mints the key and keeps it across every
retry, but only the app puts it in the request, and only the remote can act on
it. `tests/outbound.rs` in the `charpente` crate runs a workload like this one
as a swarm property: six payment jobs against
`Idempotent::header("idempotency-key", Recorder::new())`, three faulted rounds
with crashes, then the fault-free tail. Beside the three checkers it asserts
that the remote applied no charge twice, and that every recorded payment is one
the remote applied. Those two checks judge something only where a charge was
called again, so the property floors how many runs re-called a key, and how
many re-called it from a second process, rather than how many merely ran. Two
planted bugs show what the checks are for, each pinned to the seed that caught
it, and on both seeds the honest job passes with a verdict. `PayKeyless` leaves
the key header out of its call, so any re-call reaches the remote as a new
request. At seed `0xc064fe5aa31086a7` the first process's call to `/pay/2` was
applied, an injected fault ended the process before the reply settled, and the
next process called again: `double charge: pay.test applied /pay/2 2 times`. The
honest job goes through the same crash, and its second delivery is deduped. A
lost reply does the same thing inside one process: with every reply lost, the
keyless job's three attempts are three charges, where the keyed job's are one.
`PayTrusting` takes a 503 for a success, so a throttled call records a payment
the remote never made: seed `0xce5842050b68d0cb` fails with `phantom charge: job
2 recorded a payment, but pay.test never applied /pay/2`.

### `charpente test --sim`

Run it from the app crate's own directory (or pass `--dir`):

```bash
charpente test --sim [--runs N] [--seed 0x…] [--master-seed 0x…] \
    [--filter S] [--jobs N] [--format human|json] [--inputs HEX] \
    [--faults-off AXIS,…] [--dir PATH]
```

- `--runs N` — how many runs, each on a fresh seed, **per selected check**
  (default 256; must be at least 1 — a green batch of zero runs proves
  nothing). `--universes` is gone; it is an unknown flag.
- `--seed 0x…` — bypass the batch entirely and run every selected test
  once, on this exact literal seed. Coverage `require`ments are skipped (one
  run can't be expected to hit everything a batch would). This is the
  replay flag: it is exactly what a failure's own `repro:` line uses. A
  replay is one run, so it shrinks nothing.
  It prints each check as `ok`, `FAILED` or `skipped`; under a skipped check,
  `skipped:` is the reason and `detail:` the run's own detail, and the
  `sim result:` line ends with the seed.
- `--inputs HEX` — with `--seed`, replay these input choices instead of the
  ones the seed draws: each `sim.input()` draw reads the next, and every draw
  past the end reads 0. A choice above its draw's range draws what it reduces
  to, and the run records it reduced. `HEX` is each choice as an unsigned
  LEB128 number, the bytes in lowercase hex, which is what a shrunk failure's
  `repro:` line prints. Anything else (an empty value, a character that is not
  lowercase hex, an odd length, a number cut off or past 64 bits) is a usage
  error naming the flag, so an unset `--inputs "$HEX"` never replays all zeros.
- `--faults-off AXIS,…` — with `--seed`, turn these `FaultConfig` axes off
  (any field but `corruption_scope`, which scopes two axes rather than being
  one). The seed's config is sampled first and these are turned off after, so
  every other field is the seed's own. A name that is not a field is a usage
  error.
- `--master-seed 0x…` — pick where the batch's seed chain starts, instead
  of fresh OS entropy, for a byte-identical repeat of an entire batch
  (every check's every run). Cannot be combined with `--seed`. The batch's
  last line is this flag with the batch's `--runs` and `--filter`. Without
  the flag, `CHARPENTE_SIM_MASTER_SEED=0x…` in the environment picks it
  instead ("Finding a one-sided property" below); the flag wins.
- `--filter S` — run only the check named `S` when there is one, and
  otherwise every check whose name contains `S`. A failure's `repro:` line
  names its check, so it replays that check alone even when another check's
  name contains it (`charge` and `charge_twice`). Filtering happens
  **before** any seed is drawn, so a filtered batch consumes a different (but
  still fully reproducible) prefix of the seed chain than an unfiltered one
  — this is why every individual failure's repro line carries its own
  run's seed rather than relying on the batch's master seed.
- `--jobs N` — how many runs to run in parallel (default: the host's
  available parallelism). Every run's seed is pre-derived on the main
  thread, in registry order, before any work is handed out, so `--jobs 1`
  and `--jobs 8` run the identical seed sequence and produce byte-identical
  reports — worker scheduling is unobservable.
- `--format human|json` — human is the default; `json` emits one object with
  every fact the human report prints, keys in this order: `master_seed`,
  `runs`, `passed`, `failed` and `skipped` (run totals over every check),
  `coverage`, `missing_coverage` (`{check, key}` pairs), `checks`,
  `failures`, `result` and `exit`. `result` is the human result line's word
  (`ok`, `FAILED` or `nothing tested`) and `exit` the code the run exits with,
  so a record read later still says whether the batch failed, an untested
  check included. A quick pass (`CHARPENTE_SIM_FIRST_RUNS`, "Finding a
  one-sided property" below) ends on one more key, `quick_pass`, as
  `{first_runs, full_runs}`, since its `result` and `exit` judged failed runs
  only; its `runs` is the runs it ran of each check. Under `--seed`, `seed`
  stands where `master_seed` does, since the replayed seed fed back as
  `--master-seed` would run another batch. It is
  followed by `inputs`, the choices `--inputs` gave in that flag's encoding,
  when the replay was given them, and `faults_off`, the axes `--faults-off`
  turned off in field order, when it was given those. Each of `checks`, in
  registry order, is `{check, status, passed, failed, skipped, distinct_inputs,
  drew_inputs, skipped_reasons}`: `status` is `ok`, `FAILED` or `untested`
  (under `--seed` too, where the human line says `skipped`), and
  `skipped_reasons` lists every reason, uncapped, as `{reason,
  count, first_seed}` by count descending then reason, `first_seed` being the
  seed of the reason's first run in run order, plus `detail` under `--seed`,
  the one run's own text. Each of `failures` lists every failing run, in seed order, as
  `{check, seed, config, fired, message, inputs, faults_off, first_message,
  not_shrunk, replays, repro}`, with `config` carrying every fault axis. A
  check's first failure is the one a batch shrinks: its `inputs` are
  `{name, value}` pairs of the shrunk run, `message` is the shrunk run's,
  `first_message` appears only when the shrink slipped to another message,
  `replays` counts the candidates tried and `repro` replays the shrunk run.
  With shrinking off (under `charpente test --mutants`, "The timeout follows
  the scope too" below) it is its own run instead, with `not_shrunk` saying
  why and `replays` 0; no other failure carries `not_shrunk`. Every other
  failure is its own unshrunk run: its own drawn `inputs`, `faults_off` `[]`,
  `replays` 0, no `first_message`, and a `repro` that is its seed alone.
  Seeds are hex strings. The two report different totals: the human result
  line's passed and failed count checks, and the JSON's top-level `passed`,
  `failed` and `skipped` count runs, each the sum of that field over
  `checks[]`.
- `--dir PATH` — the app crate directory to build and run sim tests from
  (default: `.`). This flag is consumed by the CLI itself; every other flag
  passes straight through to the sim binary the CLI builds and execs.

Exit codes: **0** no run of any selected check failed, every `require`d
coverage key was hit, and every selected check was tested by at least one run
(skipped runs are reported, not failed — see "Giving up" above; a green batch
says how many runs tested each check); **1** at least one run failed, a
coverage requirement went unmet, or a check tested nothing because every one of
its runs was skipped (its result line then says `FAILED`, or `nothing tested`
when no check was tested) — the batch itself completed, but something in it
was red or proved nothing. A `--seed` replay replays one run per check, not a
batch, so a skipped run there exits 0, and a quick pass judges failed runs
only, so it exits 1 for a failed run and for nothing else; **2** the batch
never got that far — a build failure, no
`tests/sim.rs` target found, an unrecognized flag, `--inputs` or
`--faults-off` without `--seed` or with a value they cannot read, a filter
matching no tests, or the sim binary failing to launch or exiting by signal. Exit code
2 means "rerun once this is fixed," not "here is a reproducible bug."

### Reading a batch

A batch prints, on stderr before any run starts, the line that names its master
seed, so a batch you kill still shows the seed that reruns it:

```text
running 3 sim checks, 256 runs each, under random injected faults (master seed 0x0000000000000001)
```

